WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.819
Hey this is Presh Talwalkar. Which of the
statements, if any, are false?

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:13.799
This is a list of ten statements. The nth
statement in this list claims that exactly

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:22.080
n of the statements are false. Can you
figure it out? While you're working on

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.680
that problem, also try and figure out
this variation.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.219
It's the same list of 10 items except
the word "exactly" is replaced by the words "at least".

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.440
Give these problems a try, and when you're ready keep watching the video for the solution.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:50.000
We'll get started with the list that the nth statement says that exactly n statements are false.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.960
Consider the tenth statement in this
list.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:06.360
If statement 10 is true, that implies all of the statements in the list are false, including statement 10.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.320
This is a contradiction which means
statement 10 must be false.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.520
So we know at least one of the
statements in this list is false.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:27.030
How many of the statements can be true
at the same time?

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:39.640
The statements in the list are mutually exclusive; you cannot have exactly x statements be true and exactly y statements be true for x not equal to y.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.660
This implies at most one statement can be true.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.600
If one statement
is true that would mean nine statements

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.709
are false.

00:01:50.709 --> 00:01:55.810
So our candidate is we have nine false
statements, one true statement.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:01.509
This corresponds to the claim of
statement nine. If statements one through

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:08.400
eight, and 10 are false and statement
nine is true, that actually works out logically.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:16.100
So that's our answer. Statement nine is true and statements one through eight, and statement 10 are false.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.120
We have nine false statements and one
true statement.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:27.160
Now let's solve the variation where the
nth statement says at least n

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:33.579
statements are false. We'll proceed
similarly. We'll consider the 10th statement.

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:38.380
If this is true, that means all
statements in the list are false

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.049
including statement 10.

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:44.440
This again is a contradiction.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:49.920
So we know that statement 10 is false , and that means at least one statement must be false.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:54.010
So if at least one statement is false,

00:02:54.639 --> 00:02:57.970
we know that the first statement has to
be true.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:09.959
Now we proceed to the next statement. If
statement nine is true,  that would mean

00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:14.340
statements 2 through 9 have to be false
because we already know statement one is

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.799
true. Therefore statement 9 has to be
false, and that implies at least two

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:23.099
statements are false, statement nine and
ten are false

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:29.159
so we know that statement two is true. We can similarly proceed to show that

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:34.470
statements eight, seven, and six are false while three, four, and, five must be true.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.390
So what we end up with is we have five
possibly true statements and five

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:43.920
possibly false statements. And this turns
out to logically work, so statements

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.310
1 through 5 are true, and statements 6 through 10 are false.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.890
Did you figure it out?

